---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

/**
 * Written for a parent with no prior exposure to Scouting. Everything here is
 * general to Scouting America's Cub Scout program, not specific to Pack 662,
 * so it is safe to state plainly. Anything true only of this pack lives on
 * /meetings or /join behind a placeholder.
 */

const ranks = [
  { rank: 'Lion', grade: 'Kindergarten' },
  { rank: 'Tiger', grade: 'First grade' },
  { rank: 'Wolf', grade: 'Second grade' },
  { rank: 'Bear', grade: 'Third grade' },
  { rank: 'Webelos', grade: 'Fourth grade' },
  { rank: 'Arrow of Light', grade: 'Fifth grade' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Cub Scouting"
  description="What Cub Scouting is, how a pack and a den differ, what a meeting looks like, and which rank goes with which grade."
>
  <div class="wrap stack">
    <div class="prose">
      <h1>About Cub Scouting</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        If nobody in your family has done this before, here is the whole thing in a
        few minutes.
      </p>

      <h2>What it is</h2>
      <p>
        Cub Scouting is Scouting America's program for children in kindergarten
        through fifth grade. Kids learn practical things by doing them: cooking,
        building, first aid, getting around outdoors, taking care of the place they
        live. It is deliberately hands on, and it is designed to be done with a
        parent or another adult from the child's family rather than dropped off.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cub Scouting is open to boys and girls. Children who finish the program move
        on to Scouts BSA, the program for middle and high school age.
      </p>

      <h2>A den and a pack are not the same thing</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>den</strong> is the small group your child works in week to week,
        usually six to eight kids in the same grade with one or two adult leaders.
        This is where most of the actual activity happens.
      </p>
      <p>
        A <strong>pack</strong> is all the dens together. Pack meetings bring
        everyone into one room to recognize what kids have earned, do something as a
        whole group, and sort out what is coming next. Siblings and grandparents
        are welcome at pack meetings.
      </p>

      <h2>What a meeting is like</h2>
      <p>
        A den meeting runs about an hour. It opens with the Scout Oath and Law, moves
        into an activity tied to whatever the den is working on, and ends with word
        about what to bring next time. Kids are moving for most of it. Nobody sits
        and listens for an hour, because that is not how seven-year-olds work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pack meetings are larger, louder, and shorter on instruction. Expect awards,
        a skit or a game, and announcements.
      </p>

      <h2>Ranks by grade</h2>
      <p>
        A Scout works on one rank per school year, matched to the grade they are in.
        There is no catching up to do if you start late.
      </p>
      <table>
        <caption class="meta">Cub Scout ranks and the grade each one goes with</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Rank</th>
            <th scope="col">Grade</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            ranks.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{r.rank}</th>
                <td>{r.grade}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>What is expected of parents</h2>
      <p>
        At minimum, coming along. Lion and Tiger age Scouts are required to have an
        adult partner with them at everything. Older dens are less strict about it,
        but a pack runs entirely on volunteer parents, and packs where no parent
        steps up do not last.
      </p>
      <p>
        Any adult who works with youth takes Scouting America's Youth Protection
        training, and no adult is ever alone with a child who is not their own.
      </p>

      <h2>What it costs</h2>
      <p>
        There is a national membership fee set by Scouting America, a council fee,
        and whatever the pack charges for its own program. Uniforms and books are
        separate. Our numbers are on the <a href="/join">join page</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Still have a question? The <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> covers the ones we hear most,
        or email <a href={`mailto:${site.email.pack}`}>{site.email.pack}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
